Welcome to notebook eleven. The previous notebook gave you generics. The ability to abstract over types. This notebook gives you the partner mechanism. A way for the compiler to provide values for those types automatically, based on type alone. That is what givens are. Combined with extension methods, they unlock the type class style of programming. Adding capabilities to types without modifying their source.

If you have used Scala two, this is the modern rework of implicit val, implicit def, and implicit class. The mechanism underneath is the same. The keywords are clearer, the failure modes are more constrained, and the resulting code reads more like intent. If you have not seen the implicits world before, even better. Start fresh with the Scala three names.

The plan for this notebook is five steps. First, givens. Declare a value the compiler can find by its type. Second, using parameters. Receive a given without naming it at the call site. Third, type classes. The design pattern that gives this mechanism its power. Fourth, extension methods. Add methods to types you do not own. Fifth, implicit conversions. The controlled descendant of Scala two's implicit def, used sparingly.

Let us start with the motivation. Most non trivial functions take two kinds of inputs. The data they operate on, and contextual values they need to do the job. A logger. An execution context. A database connection. A comparison strategy. Pass the contextual values explicitly through every call and your signatures bloat. Reach for global singletons and you lose testability. Givens are a middle path. You declare the contextual value somewhere visible, mark the receiving parameter with the using keyword, and the compiler threads the value through for you. The dependency stays in the type signature, so the function is still honest about what it needs, but you stop typing it at every call.

So what is a given. A given declaration is an ordinary value with one extra property. The compiler indexes it by its type and can look it up automatically when something asks for that type. The simplest form is a one liner that names a value and its type. For example, you can write given default greeting, colon String, equals the string hello. That puts a String into given scope. Anything that asks for a String via using finds it. The name default greeting is mostly for documentation. Lookup is by type, not by name. If you write given String equals hello with no name, the compiler invents one. The name only matters if you need to refer to the given explicitly, for instance to import it.

Now the receiving side. Using parameters. A parameter list introduced with the using keyword tells the compiler, do not make me pass these explicitly. Find them by type in the enclosing scope. So you write def greet, taking a name of type String, then a separate parameter list with using greeting colon String, returning a string that combines the two. When you call greet of alice, the compiler supplies the given hello automatically. The result is hello comma alice. You can also override at the call site by writing greet of bob with an extra using howdy. The result is howdy comma bob. Two things to register. The using parameter list is a separate parameter list. It comes after the regular arguments. And you can always pass it explicitly with using value at the call site. The mechanism is opt in inference, not magic.

Sometimes you need to fetch a given inside a function body without declaring a using parameter for it. The built in summon function does exactly that. Summon, square bracket T, says, give me whatever value of type T is in given scope. So with the same default greeting in scope, val current colon String equals summon of String returns hello. This is the Scala three equivalent of Scala two's implicitly function. Summon is rarely needed in everyday code. Using parameters cover the common case. It is most useful inside generic helpers and inside given definitions that depend on other givens. We will see it shortly.

A reasonable first reaction here is, is this not just dressed up globals. It is not, for two reasons. First, lookup is type directed. By type, not by name. So you can have a given Logger, a given Clock, and a given Config in the same scope without collision. The compiler picks the one matching the type each using asks for. Second, givens are scoped. They are values bound to the scope they are declared in. They participate in normal import rules. If you do not import the given, it is not available. Which means tests can swap in their own givens without rewriting production code. These two properties are what turn a small language feature into the foundation of type classes, which is the next stop.

A type class is a way to attach behaviour to a type from the outside, without modifying that type's source. In java or python, the equivalent is making the type implement an interface, but that requires owning the type. Type classes work even for types you do not own. Like Int, String, or someone else's library class.

The recipe has three steps. Let us build a small Show type class that gives any type a show method. Like the standard toString, but type safe and instance by instance.

Step one. Declare a generic trait describing the capability. You write trait Show of A, with a single abstract method, def show, taking an a of type A, returning a String. One trait, one parameter, one method.

Step two. For each concrete type you want to support, provide a given that supplies an implementation. These are the type class instances. So you write given Show of Int with, then on the next indented line, def show of a equals a dot toString. Similarly, given Show of String with, def show of a equals quote a quote with escapes. And given Show of Boolean with, def show of a equals if a then yes else no. The with keyword introduces an anonymous given whose body provides the trait's methods. Each one is a separate value indexed by its full type. Show of Int, Show of String, Show of Boolean. They live as siblings. None of them collide because they have different parameter types.

Step three. Write polymorphic code that uses the instances. You write def render, square bracket A, taking an a of type A, in a using parameter list taking s of type Show of A, returning a string by calling s dot show of a. Now you can call render of forty two and get the string forty two. Render of the string scala and get the quoted string scala back. Render of true and get yes. What is happening at each call. The compiler sees render of forty two, infers A as Int, then looks for a given Show of Int in scope, finds the one we declared, and threads it in. From your code's perspective, you wrote one polymorphic function and got per type behaviour for free.

Asking for an unnamed using parameter of type F of A is so common that Scala has a one character shorthand. Square bracket A colon Show, called a context bound, means there is some A, and there must be a given Show of A available. Inside the body, you fetch it with summon. So def render, square bracket A colon Show, taking an a of type A, returning summon of Show of A dot show of a, is exactly equivalent to the verbose form with using. The two forms are interchangeable. Use the long form when you need to name the instance for repeated use. Use the context bound when you only need it once via summon.

Type classes give you functions that work over many types. But the call syntax is render of x, not x dot render. Sometimes the second reads better, especially when chaining. Extension methods let you attach a method to an existing type from outside, without modifying its source.

You write extension, then in parentheses s colon String, and then on indented lines a series of method definitions. Like def shout, returning s dot to upper case followed by an exclamation mark. Or def words, returning s dot split on whitespace dot to list. Now you can call the string hello dot shout and get back HELLO. Or call one two three dot words and get back a list of three strings. The extension header introduces a receiver s of type String. Every def inside the block becomes a method callable on any String. The compiler rewrites the dot call into the equivalent function call under the hood. No magic, just sugar.

Extensions can take their own type parameters and even depend on givens. This is the form you will use most when designing type class APIs. You write extension, square bracket A, then in parentheses a of type A, then a using parameter list with s of type Show of A. Inside the block, def show returning s dot show of a. Now any value at all, as long as there is a given Show of A in scope, gains a dot show method. Forty two dot show returns the string forty two. Scala dot show returns the quoted string. True dot show returns yes. The dot show form is just the method call form of render. Same lookup, same behaviour, friendlier syntax. This is exactly how libraries like Cats give you triple equals, the combine pipe operator, and the traverse method, as if they were native methods on every type.

Givens can take using parameters of their own. The compiler chains them. Ask for a Show of List of Int, and if there is a given Show of Int plus a given Show of List of A that requires a given Show of A, the compiler assembles the instance for you. So you write given list show, square bracket A, with a using parameter s of type Show of A, returning Show of List of A, with the body, def show taking a list, mapping each element through s dot show, and joining them with brackets and commas. Now render of List of one comma two comma three returns the bracketed string one comma two comma three. Render of a list of strings returns a list of quoted strings. And render of a list of lists, like List of List of one comma List of two comma three, returns a nested bracket structure. Read that last call carefully. The compiler resolves Show of List of List of Int by stacking the list show instance twice on top of Show of Int. You wrote two givens. You got behaviour for every nested list type for free. This compositionality is what makes type classes scale to large libraries.

Where should you declare your givens. Two locations matter, and the compiler looks in both automatically. First, the companion object of the type class. Put your standard instances on object Show and they are found whenever someone asks for any Show of A. No import needed. Second, the companion object of the data type. Put given Show of Order on object Order and it is found whenever someone asks for Show of Order. No import needed either. Anything declared anywhere else needs an explicit import to come into given scope at the call site. The standard import for given values is import obj dot given, to import all givens, or import obj dot the curly brace given Show of Int, to import a specific one.

You have actually already used type classes in the standard library without realising. Two examples. The first is Ordering. The type class behind sorting and comparison. When you write List of three comma one comma two dot sorted, you get back List of one comma two comma three. When you call sorted on a list of strings, it sorts alphabetically. The signature of sorted is roughly, taking a using parameter ord of type Ordering of A, returning a list. Different types sort differently because each provides its own given Ordering. You can also supply a custom one. Writing given Ordering of String equals Ordering dot by the length method, and then sorting a list of strings, sorts them by length instead of alphabetically. The second example is Numeric. Every time you call dot sum or dot max or dot min on a numeric collection, the compiler is silently supplying a Numeric or Ordering instance from given scope. That is why a list of integers dot sum works, and a list of options dot sum does not. There is no Numeric of Option of Int in scope.

Scala three keeps automatic value conversions, but locks them behind an explicit type. Conversion, of From and To. Declaring one as a given lets the compiler insert it where a From is supplied but a To is expected. So with import scala dot language dot implicit conversions at the top of the file, and given Conversion of Int and String equals the to string method, you can write val s colon String equals forty two and the compiler inserts the conversion for you. The import is required at the call site. Scala three makes you opt in deliberately. That import is the visible warning sign in a code review.

Use these sparingly. Most of the time when you think you want a conversion, you actually want an extension method or an explicit dot to call. Conversions blur the type system. A String parameter quietly accepting an Int is exactly the kind of thing that turns into a long bug hunt years later. Reserve them for true equivalence cases, like numeric widening or library interoperability, where the conversion is genuinely information preserving.

A short list of pitfalls you will hit at first. First, the error message no given instance of type X was found. The compiler could not find a value to fill the using. Either declare one, or import it from where it lives. The error always names the exact type it was looking for. Second, ambiguous givens. If two given Show of Int are in scope, the compiler refuses to choose. Move one out, or import only the one you want. Third, forgetting the with keyword or the equals sign in a given definition. Both forms are valid. Given Show of Int with, then methods, or given Show of Int equals new instance with methods. Missing the connector is a common typo. Fourth, putting instances in a random file. Givens that are not in a companion object only count when explicitly imported. A package level given in a deep file is easy to miss.

Let us close with a realistic miniature. A JSON encoder. Define a JsonEncoder type class, provide instances for primitives, derive an instance for List, and add a method style syntax. Trait JsonEncoder of A, with one method, encode, taking an a of A, returning a String. On the companion object of JsonEncoder, given JsonEncoder of Int with, def encode returning a dot toString. Given JsonEncoder of String with, def encode returning the quoted string. Given JsonEncoder of Boolean with, def encode returning a dot toString. Then a derived instance. Given list encoder, square bracket A, with a using parameter e of type JsonEncoder of A, returning JsonEncoder of List of A, with the body mapping each element through e dot encode and joining with brackets and commas. Outside the companion, extension, square bracket A, taking an a of type A, with a using parameter e of type JsonEncoder of A, defining def to json returning e dot encode of a. Now forty two dot to json returns the string forty two. Hello dot to json returns the quoted string. A list of integers dot to json returns the bracketed JSON array. And a list of lists of strings dot to json returns the nested bracket structure, fully encoded.

Read that example end to end and notice the layering. The trait describes the capability. Instances live on the companion object of the type class, so they are found automatically. The list encoder given takes another given as a using parameter, which lets the compiler derive encoders for lists of anything that already has one. The extension method gives you the dot to json syntax. None of Int, String, or List knows anything about JSON, yet they all gain the method through the type class machinery.

Notebook twelve covers advanced types. Opaque types, type aliases, union and intersection types, match types, and a closer look at variance and bounds. Together with this notebook's givens and extensions, those are the tools that let Scala libraries express precise constraints in types while keeping the day to day usage syntax clean.
